/* How many threads this process is running. `[[package.providers]]` selects
 * the host's count: one /proc/self/task entry per thread on linux, the
 * kernel's task info on macOS. Forking is safe only from a single-threaded
 * process, since a lock another thread held at the fork stays held forever in
 * the child, so the forked worker pool asks before it starts. */
class ProcessThreads {
	/* Threads parked in a join for the rest of the process's work. Only the
	 * parking thread changes it, before it creates the thread it joins and
	 * after the join, so the thread creation and join order every access. */
	class int parkedThreads = 0;

	/* The live thread count, the caller's included, or -1 when the host
	 * cannot tell. */
	class int count() { return ProcessThreadsProvider.count(); }

	/* The live threads a fork must account for: the count less parked
	 * threads, or -1 when the host cannot tell. A parked thread blocks in a
	 * join holding no lock, so it leaves nothing held in a child. */
	class int unparkedCount() {
		int live = ProcessThreadsProvider.count();
		if (live < 0) { return live; }
		return live - ProcessThreads.parkedThreads;
	}

	/* The calling thread is about to block in a join that holds no lock until
	 * the thread it joins finishes. Call before creating that thread. */
	class void park() { ProcessThreads.parkedThreads = ProcessThreads.parkedThreads + 1; }

	/* The parked thread has resumed, or never parked. */
	class void unpark() { ProcessThreads.parkedThreads = ProcessThreads.parkedThreads - 1; }

	/* A forked child runs only the forking thread: nothing it inherited is
	 * parked. */
	class void forgetParked() { ProcessThreads.parkedThreads = 0; }
}
